    "result_text": "Terms now count business days, and `bin/e2e` passes: all 4 tests in `e2e/specs/acceptance.spec.ts` are green. The new V46 migration also runs cleanly when the app starts.\n\n**How a due-by is worked out now**\n- Each claim stores two new things: the date its due-by counts from (`due_from`) and the terms it was fixed with (`terms`).\n- Its due-by is the terms-th business day after `due_from`, skipping Saturdays, Sundays and the sheet's holidays. Terms of 0 make it due on its own date. The counting is in `holiday/BusinessDays.java`.\n- When a claim is added or split, the terms in force on its date are fixed on it and the due-by is counted with the holidays on the sheet at that moment.\n- Adding a holiday now re-works every due-by on the sheet, using each claim's own fixed terms. Any change to the overdue totals is audited the same way as other changes.\n- Overdue, the late charge's boundaries and the period a claim belongs to all read the stored due-by, so they follow without other changes.\n\n**Existing claims (V46 migration)**\nEach claim already stored counts from its date. Its terms are taken from the due-by it showed: the days from its date to its due-by, minus any holidays the old rule pushed the due-by past. Those terms are then counted as business days. This is a best guess, because the old data never recorded the terms; if a claim had been re-dated, the guess will be off. It's a Java migration because the counting needs code, and it reuses `BusinessDays`.\n\n**Decision for you:** a claim's due-by still counts from the date it was added with. Changing the claim's date later (re-date or edit) doesn't move it, as before. The tests require this: one claim's date is edited from 2026-03-15 to 2026-02-25 and it must still be due 2026-04-02. Counting from the new date would be a small change, but those tests would then fail.\n\n**Not handled or not tested**\n- Adding a holiday doesn't refresh the claims already on screen; the new due-bys show on the next sheet load.\n- A holiday is never refused, even when it moves a claim's due-by into or out of a closed period.\n- The tests only add holidays before any claims, so re-working due-bys after a later holiday isn't covered by them.\n\nI also changed the terms labels and comments on the settings and person screens from \"days\" to \"business days\".",
